## Overview

`qr-manager-api` is a generic short-URL / QR code redirect service. Each record has:

- a server-allocated 4-character slug (lowercase alphanumeric, `nanoid`-backed)
- a mutable target URL
- a logical type (`iot-device`, `plant`, `other`, …) and a label

Calling apps (e.g. `iot-management-api`, future plant database) create a QR record, store the returned slug, and print the rendered QR image. Whenever the target URL changes, only the database row is updated — the printed QR keeps working.

### How a scan resolves

```
phone scans QR
    │  (URL encoded into the image: `${redirect.baseURL}/${slug}`)
    ▼
GET ${redirect.baseURL}/${slug}            ← this API
    │
    ▼  302 Location: <targetURL>           ← whatever's in the DB row
phone follows redirect to <targetURL>
```

`targetURL` is free-form. It can point to anything reachable from the scanning device — your internal app, an external site, a Google Maps link, a YouTube video — and you can change it at any time without reprinting the QR.

Swagger UI is available at the root path (`/`) once the server is running.

## Quick Start

### Docker Compose

```yaml
services:
  qr-manager-api:
    image: ghcr.io/radoslavirha/qr-manager-api:latest
    ports:
      - "4011:4011"
    environment:
      NODE_ENV: production
      MONGODB_URL: mongodb://mongo:27017/qr-manager
      MONGODB_USER: qr-manager
      MONGODB_PASS: secret
      QR_REDIRECT_BASE_URL: https://qr.home
    volumes:
      - ./config:/home/app/config:ro
    restart: unless-stopped
  mongo:
    image: mongo:7
    restart: unless-stopped
```

The API expects a `config/production.json` (or environment variables — see [Configuration](#configuration)).

## Configuration

Configuration is loaded from `config/<NODE_ENV>.json`. The minimal production config:

```json
{
  "server": { "httpPort": 4011 },
  "mongodb": {
    "enabled": true,
    "url": "mongodb://mongo:27017/qr-manager",
    "connectionOptions": { "user": "qr-manager", "pass": "secret" }
  },
  "redirect": {
    "baseURL": "https://qr.home"
  }
}
```

Every JSON value can be overridden by an environment variable via `config/custom-environment-variables.json`:

| Env var | JSON path |
| --- | --- |
| `MONGODB_URL` | `mongodb.url` |
| `MONGODB_USER` | `mongodb.connectionOptions.user` |
| `MONGODB_PASS` | `mongodb.connectionOptions.pass` |
| `QR_REDIRECT_BASE_URL` | `redirect.baseURL` |

### Reference

| Key | Type | Required | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `server.httpPort` | number | yes | HTTP server port. |
| `mongodb.enabled` | boolean | yes | Must be `true` in production. |
| `mongodb.url` | string | when enabled | MongoDB connection string. |
| `mongodb.connectionOptions.user` | string | optional | MongoDB username. |
| `mongodb.connectionOptions.pass` | string | optional | MongoDB password. |
| `redirect.baseURL` | string | yes | Public host where this API serves `GET /:slug`. Combined with the generated slug to compose the URL embedded in printed QR images (e.g. `https://qr.home/x7k2`). Distinct from per-record `targetURL` (free-form, stored in DB and resolved at scan time — can point to google, an internal app, anything). |
| `logger.level` | string | optional | Pino-style log level (default `info`). |
| `otel.*` | object | optional | OpenTelemetry exporter configuration. |

## REST API

Swagger UI is served at `http://<host>:<httpPort>/`.

All requests/responses use `application/json` (image endpoint excepted).

### Public redirect

#### `GET /:slug`

Resolves a 4-character slug. Returns:

- `302 Found` with `Location: <target_url>` when the record exists and `active = true`
- `404 Not Found` for an unknown slug, a record with `active = false`, or a path that does not match `[a-z0-9]{4}`

This is the URL physically printed on the QR sticker. Keep it short and stable.

### Admin

#### `POST /qr-codes`

Allocates a new slug and persists the mapping.

**Request**

```json
{
  "targetURL": "https://iot-ui.home/devices/shelf-1",
  "label": "Shelf 1 — Plant Watering",
  "type": "iot-device"
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `targetURL` | string | URL the slug will redirect to. |
| `label` | string | Human readable name. |
| `type` | enum | `iot-device`, `plant`, `other`. |

**Response** `201`

```json
{
  "id": "671b00000000000000000001",
  "slug": "x7k2",
  "targetURL": "https://iot-ui.home/devices/shelf-1",
  "label": "Shelf 1 — Plant Watering",
  "type": "iot-device",
  "active": true,
  "qrURL": "https://qr.home/x7k2",
  "imageURL": "https://api.server.home/qr/qr-codes/671b00000000000000000001/image",
  "createdAt": "2026-04-26T10:00:00.000Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-04-26T10:00:00.000Z"
}
```

#### `GET /qr-codes`

Lists QR codes. Supported filters:

| Query | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `type` | enum | Filter by type. |
| `active` | boolean | `true` / `false`. |

**Response** `200`

```json
{
  "items": [ { "...": "..." } ]
}
```

#### `GET /qr-codes/:id`

Returns a single record by Mongo `id`. `404` when not found.

#### `PUT /qr-codes/:id`

Updates the mutable fields. All fields are optional; omitted fields are unchanged.

```json
{
  "targetURL": "https://new-ui.home/devices/shelf-1",
  "label": "Shelf 1 (renamed)",
  "type": "iot-device",
  "active": false
}
```

#### `DELETE /qr-codes/:id`

Deletes the record. The slug becomes available for reuse. `204 No Content` on success.

#### `GET /qr-codes/:id/image?format=svg|png&size=<px>&ecLevel=L|M|Q|H`

Returns the rendered QR image encoding `${redirect.baseURL}/${slug}`.

| Query | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `format` | `svg` | `svg` = vector (best for print); `png` = raster |
| `size` | qrcode lib default | PNG width in px. Range 64–4096. Ignored for SVG. |
| `ecLevel` | `M` (~15% damage tolerance) | `L` (~7%), `Q` (~25%), `H` (~30%). Higher = more redundancy = larger module count = bigger print. |

##### Sizing the printed QR

Two factors drive the physical print size:

1. **URL length** — the slug is fixed at 4 chars, but `redirect.baseURL` matters. `https://qr.home/x7k2` (19 chars) is much smaller than `http://192.168.1.195:4011/x7k2` (30 chars). For production set up a short DNS name (CNAME `qr.home` → cluster ingress).
2. **Error correction** — `M` is the default. Use `L` for the smallest possible print on protected indoor labels; bump to `Q`/`H` for outdoor or abused labels.

Approximate module counts (byte mode, 4-char slug):

| URL length | L | M | Q | H |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 19 chars (`qr.home`) | 25×25 (V2) | 25×25 (V2) | 25×25 (V2) | 29×29 (V3) |
| 30 chars (raw IP+port) | 25×25 (V2) | 29×29 (V3) | 29×29 (V3) | 33×33 (V4) |

##### Print size guide (Prusa MK4S, 0.4mm nozzle)

- **Absolute minimum:** ~10mm with `qr.home` + `L`. Likely fragile.
- **Recommended:** **15–20mm** with `qr.home` + `M`. Reliable scan with phone cameras, single-nozzle prints fine.
- **Outdoor / abused:** 25mm+ with `H`.

Print as a flat top layer with colour swap (multi-material) or paint-fill the recessed dark modules. Use the SVG download for laser-cut or vector-toolpath workflows; use the PNG download (`size=1024` recommended) when the slicer expects a raster image.

